WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Multiprocessing.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.596
When the work is pure computation, threads can't help.

3
00:00:05.596 --> 00:00:07.000
Separate processes can.

4
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:13.033
A process is a separate Python interpreter, with its own memory and its own GIL.

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.933
That's how Python gets true parallelism across CPU cores.

6
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:22.400
It's the tool for CPU-bound work, like feature extraction and number crunching.

7
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.833
Processes don't share memory, so data has to travel.

8
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.800
It starts in your main process.

9
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.200
Each argument is pickled: serialized and shipped.

10
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.433
A worker computes on its own core.

11
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.800
Then the result is pickled and shipped back.

12
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.333
So when is it worth it?

13
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.490
Heavy work per item, like an FFT per light curve, wins big.

14
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:48.067
A trivial square loses to a plain loop, even over ten million numbers.

15
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:50.533
Startup eats the gain on small jobs.

16
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:54.286
The rule: the work per item must dwarf the pickling cost.

17
00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:56.467
List length alone doesn't decide it.

18
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:58.867
Here it is in code.

19
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.733
The function receives a file path and loads the array itself.

20
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:08.767
The main guard is required on macOS and Windows, because workers re-import your module.

21
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:12.567
Then Pool dot map splits the paths across eight processes.

22
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:15.000
Now a real job.

23
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.703
Your exoplanet classifier needs FFT features from a hundred thousand light curves.

24
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:23.733
Pure CPU work, where threads would be useless.

25
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:29.767
Eight processes, with workers loading their own arrays, so almost nothing pays the pickling tax.

26
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.200
Fifty five minutes drops to about eight.

27
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.138
Let's recap.

28
00:01:34.138 --> 00:01:36.600
Processes have their own memory and GIL.

29
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.133
Use them for CPU-bound work.

30
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:46.631
Startup and pickling cost real time, so the work per item must be heavy, and you should send paths, not arrays.

31
00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:47.733
Next up: asyncio.
